🔹 Step 1: Start with the brute force idea
You want the max area between two vertical lines.
Brute force: check every pair (i, j). That’s O(n²). Too slow.
So you think: “Can I move pointers smartly instead of checking everything?”

🔹 Step 2: Notice the limiting factor
For any pair (left, right), area = (right - left) × min(height[left], height[right]).
The shorter wall is always the limiting factor.
If you move the taller wall inward, width shrinks but height doesn’t increase → area gets worse.
So you must move the pointer at the shorter wall.

🔹 Step 3: Realize the width shrinks
Every inward move reduces width.
To get a bigger area, you need a taller wall to compensate.
If the new wall is ≤ h, then height doesn’t increase → area shrinks.
So you don’t even need to check those walls.

